{"item_id": "fresh_N363462", "reference_source": "ruling:N363462", "product": "Item 1796150, which you have referred to as a laundry hauler, is a tote bag constructed with an outer surface of plastic sheeting. It features casters and four wheels. It has inner foam and a zipper closure. It has three handles: one handle on the right side, and two handles on the front and back sides. The dimensions are 18” L x 14” W x 24” H.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363462, gold code 4202.92.4500:\n has inner foam and a zipper closure. It has three handles: one handle on the right side, and two handles on the front and back sides. The dimensions are 18” L x 14” W x 24” H. The binding ruling request seeks classification of item 1796150 in subheading 9403.89.6015, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Other furniture and parts thereof: Furniture of other materials, including cane, osier, bamboo or similar materials: Other: Other: Other household.” We disagree. The subject merchandise is more specifically provided for elsewhere in the tariff schedule. The applicable subheading for the tote bag will be 4202.92.4500, HTSUS, which provides for travel, sports, and similar bags, with outer surface of sheeting of plastic, other. The general rate of duty will be 20 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Vikki Lazaro at vikki.lazaro@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4202.92.45.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 4202 covers traveling bags, shopping bags, tote bags and similar containers of sheeting of plastics", "GRI 1: Chapter 63 laundry bags (6307) excluded because the outer surface is plastic sheeting, not textile; containers of 4202 type named in heading", "GRI 6: 4202.92 other, outer surface of sheeting of plastics or textile; travel, sports and similar bags; 4202.92.45 other (outer surface of plastic sheeting)"], "rationale": "The laundry hauler is a tote bag, a container of the kind covered by heading 4202, with an outer surface of plastic sheeting. Wheels and casters do not change its character as a tote/travel-type bag. CBP consistently places tote bags with an outer surface of plastic sheeting in 4202.92.4500 (e.g., HQ H278965, in force). It is therefore classified in 4202.92.45.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6307.90.98", "reason": "textile laundry bags; this bag has an outer surface of plastic sheeting and is a 4202 tote-type container"}, {"code": "4202.92.31", "reason": "outer surface of man-made fiber textile; here outer surface is plastic sheeting"}, {"code": "4202.12", "reason": "trunks/suitcases subheading; item is a tote bag, not a suitcase"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363468", "reference_source": "ruling:N363468", "product": "The merchandise under consideration, identified by model number DSPx 2.4, is described as a digital signal processor (DSP) used within high-performance loudspeaker systems. The subject device is comprised of various electronic components, including an onboard DSP chipset, enclosed within a metal housing. The front of the device includes a rotary dial, while the back is equipped with various input and output connections. These include analog audio, i.e. RCA and XLR, digital audio, i.e. S/PDIF, as well as wireless connectivity. You state that the device is designed to receive and process an audio signal from an input source. This processing includes the conversion of a digital signal to analog to be used by various outputs, such as speakers, tweeter, subwoofers, etc. Additionally, the device can divide the frequencies, perform equalization, and adjust levels across various channels. We note that the device does not perform any amplification of sound.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363468, gold code 8543.70.9100:\n back is equipped with various input and output connections. These include analog audio, i.e. RCA and XLR, digital audio, i.e. S/PDIF, as well as wireless connectivity. You state that the device is designed to receive and process an audio signal from an input source. This processing includes the conversion of a digital signal to analog to be used by various outputs, such as speakers, tweeter, subwoofers, etc. Additionally, the device can divide the frequencies, perform equalization, and adjust levels across various channels. We note that the device does not perform any amplification of sound. The applicable subheading for the DSP unit, model number DSPx 2.4, will be 8543.70.9100, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Electrical machines and apparatus, having individual functions, not specified or included elsewhere in this chapter; parts thereof: Other machines and apparatus: Other: Other: Other: Digital signal processing apparatus capable of connecting to a wired or wireless network for the mixing of sound.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Luke LePage at l", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8543.70.98.60", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: not an amplifier (8518.40) or loudspeaker (8518.21/.22) since it performs no amplification and emits no sound", "GRI 1: not a part of loudspeakers under Section XVI note 2 because it is a separate apparatus with an individual function; heading 8543 covers electrical apparatus having individual functions not elsewhere specified", "GRI 6: 8543.70 other machines and apparatus; 8543.70.98 other (not a DSP apparatus for mixing of sound on a network); 8543.70.98.60 other (not an amplifier or effects pedal)"], "rationale": "The DSP receives audio signals and performs D/A conversion, crossover, equalization and level control but performs no amplification, so it is not an audio-frequency amplifier of 8518.40. It is an electrical apparatus with an individual function not specified elsewhere in chapter 85, falling in 8543.70. CBP classified a similar digital-to-analog audio control unit without amplification in 8543 (J80321, in force). The residual line 8543.70.98.60 applies unless the device is a networked sound-mixing DSP.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8518.40.20", "reason": "device does not amplify audio signals"}, {"code": "8518.90", "reason": "not a part of a loudspeaker; it is a standalone signal processor with its own function (Section XVI note 2(a) sends goods of a specific heading to that heading)"}, {"code": "8543.70.91.00", "reason": "that line covers networked DSP apparatus for the mixing of sound; this device divides frequencies/equalizes rather than mixes multiple sources (arguable)"}, {"code": "8543.70.98.10", "reason": "not an amplifier"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363486", "reference_source": "ruling:N363486", "product": "You submitted a sample of an adult size costume identified as Olympian Goddess, item number WMT5027-027, which consists of a sleeveless pullover dress with attached drape, two pin-on shoulder medallions and two armbands. The attached drape, back ties, and hem bottom have raw, unfinished edges.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363486, gold code 9505.90.6000:\nal Rule of Interpretation 3(b) is applicable when goods are, prima facie, classifiable under two or more headings, and have been put up in sets for retail sale. GRI 3(b) states that the goods “shall be classified as if they consisted of the material or component which gives them their essential character.” The flimsy man-made knitted textile dress provides the essential character of the set and features edges on the neck and armholes that are folded over once and sewn with a single line of loose, long straight stitches. The attached drape, back ties, and hem bottom have raw, unfinished edges. The applicable subheading for the Olympian Goddess costume, item number WMT5027-027 will be 9505.90.6000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Festive, carnival or other entertainment articles, including magic tricks, and practical joke articles; parts and accessories thereof: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. Please note that separate Federal Trade Commission marking requirements exist regarding country of origin, fiber content, and other information that must appear on many textile items. You should contact the Federal Trade Commission, Division of Enforcement, 600 Pennsylvania Avenue, N.W., Washington, D.C., 20580, for information on the applicability of these requirements to this item. Information can also be found on the FTC website www.ftc.gov. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of th", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9505.90.60.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: Chapter 95 note 1 excludes fancy dress of textile materials of Chapter 61/62, but per CBP practice (Rubie's) flimsy, non-durable costumes with raw unfinished edges are festive articles of heading 9505", "GRI 3(b): costume set put up together; the dress gives essential character", "GRI 6: 9505.90 other festive articles; 9505.90.60.00 other"], "rationale": "The costume dress has raw, unfinished edges at the drape, back ties and hem, making it a flimsy, non-durable festive article rather than wearing apparel. CBP classifies such goddess-type costumes in 9505.90.6000 (N327404 Love Goddess, in force; N324994 Golden Goddess). The accessories (medallions, armbands) are packed with the dress and follow it as a set. Classification is 9505.90.60.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6204.49 / 6104.4x", "reason": "costume is flimsy with raw unfinished edges and is not wearing apparel of chapters 61/62"}, {"code": "9505.90.40", "reason": "that line covers Halloween/festive masks and other specified items, not textile costumes"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363487", "reference_source": "ruling:N363487", "product": "The M-ELI1, M-ELI1P, M-ELIK1P, and M-ELI2 series hydraulic units are known as Marzocchi Pompes. Each unit features an aluminum casing equipped with an inlet, an outlet, a flange cover, housing bearings and two gears with shafts. When driven by an external motor, the unit functions as a pump; conversely, when the unit receives pressurized oil, the gear rotation can be reversed, allowing it to operate as a motor. These units vary in speed, suction range, and displacement, all of which are critical performance factors.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363487, gold code 8413.60.0030:\nas Marzocchi Pompes. Each unit features an aluminum casing equipped with an inlet, an outlet, a flange cover, housing bearings and two gears with shafts. When driven by an external motor, the unit functions as a pump; conversely, when the unit receives pressurized oil, the gear rotation can be reversed, allowing it to operate as a motor. These units vary in speed, suction range, and displacement, all of which are critical performance factors. New York Ruling N361506, dated May 27, 2026, determined that similar hydraulic units are classified under heading 8413, which provides for liquid pumps. The applicable subheading for the hydraulic units will be 8413.60.0030, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for Pumps for liquids, whether or not fitted with a measuring device; liquid elevators; part thereof: Other rotary positive displacement pumps: Hydraulic fluid power pumps: Gear type. The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Sandra Martinez at sandra.martinez@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8413.60.00.30", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8413 pumps for liquids; units are rotary positive displacement gear pumps whose speed, suction range and displacement are pump performance factors", "Section XVI note 3: machine performing more than one function classified by principal function, which is pumping", "GRI 6: 8413.60 other rotary positive displacement pumps; hydraulic fluid power pumps; 8413.60.00.30 gear type"], "rationale": "The Marzocchi units are gear pumps that can be run in reverse as motors. Under Section XVI note 3 the principal function is pumping, and speed, suction and displacement are critical pump characteristics. CBP classified identical Marzocchi Pompe gear units in 8413.60.0030 as hydraulic fluid power gear pumps (N361506, in force). The code is 8413.60.00.30.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8412.29", "reason": "hydraulic motor function is secondary; principal function is pumping (Section XVI note 3)"}, {"code": "8413.60.00.20", "reason": "vane type; these are gear type"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363542", "reference_source": "ruling:N363542", "product": "Style 5127-007 “British Redcoat” is a costume comprised of a coat, a vest, pants, a cravat, a sash, boot covers, and a hat. You state that this item will allow consumers to portray a British soldier character. The coat, which is made to men’s size specifications, is constructed of 100 percent polyester double-knit fabric that prevents the edges from fraying. The garment features a well-made collar; lapels extending from the collar to below the waist, to each of which has been stitched six non-functional buttons and strips of narrow woven fabric with a metallic gold appearance; long sleeves with well-made, turned-back cuffs, to each of which has been stitched a non-functional button and strips of narrow woven fabric with a metallic gold appearance; a back panel constructed from three pieces of fabric sewn together both horizontally and vertically; and tails extending to approximately the knee area. The front and back ends of each tail are turned back, stitched together, and finished with a non-functional button. The pullover vest features a V-styled neckline; five non-functional buttons extending along the front center that give the appearance of a full front opening; raw, unfinished armholes; and a raw, unfinished, straight back bottom. The front of the neckline features backing fabric that extends down the inner front plackets to support the buttons. The fabric of the back", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363542, gold code 6110.30.3053:\nthe boot cover's fabric. The hat is constructed of stiff, molded felt-like material of 100 percent polyester. It features a rounded, molded crown surrounded by a wide brim that is turned upward on three sides to create a tricorne shape. The outer edge of the brim is finished with textile binding. The front of the hat is embellished with a textile bow and a round, gold-colored decorative ornament suspended at the center of the bow. The coat is the only well-made article of apparel included in the costume. Further, the essential character of the costume is imparted by the coat. Accordingly, the applicable subheading for Style 5127-007 will be 6110.30.3053, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States, which provides for: Sweaters, pullovers, sweatshirts, waistcoats (vests) and similar articles, knitted or crocheted: Of man-made fibers: Other: Other: Other: Other: Other: Men’s or boys’: Other. The general rate of duty will be 32 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Maryalice Nowak at maryalice.nowak@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6103.33.20.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: coat is well made (finished collar, lapels, cuffs, non-fraying double knit), so it is wearing apparel of Chapter 61, not a flimsy festive article of 9505", "Section XI note 14 (formerly 13): textile garments of different headings are classified separately, so the costume is not a GRI 3(b) set; the first-named article, the coat, is classified", "GRI 1: tailored coat with lapels, multi-panel construction and sleeves meets the suit-type jacket criteria of heading 6103 (men's, knit)", "GRI 6: 6103.33 of synthetic fibers; 6103.33.20.00 other (not 23% or more wool)"], "rationale": "Under Section XI note 14 the garments of this costume are classified separately, so the first-named article, the coat, is classified. The coat is well made of non-fraying polyester double knit with a finished collar, lapels, turned-back cuffs and a multi-piece back, so it is wearing apparel rather than a festive article. Its tailored features point to a men's knit suit-type jacket of synthetic fibers, 6103.33.20.00. If the tailoring criteria are not met, CBP's practice for costume coats (N003904, in force) would place it in 6114.30.30.60.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9505.90.60.00", "reason": "coat is well made and durable, not flimsy/non-durable"}, {"code": "6114.30.30.60", "reason": "used by CBP for a costume coat lacking suit-type jacket tailoring (N003904); this coat has lapels, turned-back cuffs and a multi-panel back so 6103 is more specific; arguable if CBP finds suit-type criteria unmet"}, {"code": "6101.30", "reason": "not an overcoat/anorak designed for protection from the elements"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363573", "reference_source": "ruling:N363573", "product": "The items are aluminum pill cases, Mission Pill, SKU #MPC, and Mission Vitamin, SKU #MVC. The cases and lids are made from aluminum alloy designation series number 6061-T6. The aluminum lid is removable and is held in place by a stainless-steel spring-ball plunger, which retains the lid to the aluminum body. Each aluminum pill case has three internal compartments designed for pills, vitamins, or vitamin supplements. The aluminum pill cases will be sold for personal use and are intended primarily for home use. Mission Pill, SKU #MPC measures approximately 3.00 inches long by 1.75 inches wide by 0.75 inches high. Mission Vitamin, SKU #MVC measures approximately 3.75 inches long by 2.10 inches wide by 0.90 inches high. Each aluminum pill case is made of an aluminum body, an aluminum lid, a stainless-steel spring ball plunger. The aluminum constitutes the majority of weight and bulk of each pill case. It also serves the primarily function of storing and organizing the pills, vitamins, and supplements. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363573, gold code 7615.10.9100:\nIn your letter dated July 23, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. The items are aluminum pill cases, Mission Pill, SKU #MPC, and Mission Vitamin, SKU #MVC. The cases and lids are made from aluminum alloy designation series number 6061-T6. The aluminum lid is removable and is held in place by a stainless-steel spring-ball plunger, which retains the lid to the aluminum body. Each aluminum pill case has three internal compartments designed for pills, vitamins, or vitamin supplements. The aluminum pill cases will be sold for personal use and are intended primarily for home use. Mission Pill, SKU #MPC measures approximately 3.00 inches long by 1.75 inches wide by 0.75 inches high. Mission Vitamin, SKU #MVC measures approximately 3.75 inches long by 2.10 inches wide by 0.90 inches high. The Mission pill cases are considered a composite good within the meaning of General Rule of Interpretation (GRI) 3. Each aluminum pill case is made of an aluminum body, an aluminum lid, a stainless-steel spring ball plunger. The aluminum constitutes the majority of weight and bulk of each pill case. It also serves the primarily function of storing and organizing the pills, vitamins, and supplements. Therefore, it is the opinion of this office that the aluminum component provides the essential character within the meaning of GRI 3(b). Therefore, the classification of the Mission Pill, SKU #MPC, and Mission Vitamin, SKU #MVC will be subheading 7615.10.9100, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States, HTSUS, which provides for “Table, kitchen or other household articles and parts thereof, of aluminum; sanitary ware and parts thereof, of aluminum: Table, kitchen or other household articles and parts thereof; pot scourers and scouring or polishing pads, gloves and the like: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be 3.1 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "7615.10.91.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 7615 covers table, kitchen or other household articles of aluminum; the case is used primarily in the home", "GRI 3(b) (via GRI 2(b)): composite of aluminum and steel; aluminum gives essential character (majority of weight, bulk and storage function), so classified as an article of aluminum", "GRI 6: 7615.10 household articles; not pot scourers, not cooking or kitchen ware; 7615.10.91.00 other"], "rationale": "The pill cases are composite goods of aluminum and a small stainless-steel plunger; the aluminum body and lid give the essential character under GRI 3(b). Because they are sold for personal use primarily in the home, they are household articles of aluminum of heading 7615 rather than articles carried in the pocket or handbag. They are not cooking or kitchen ware, so they fall in 7615.10.91.00. CBP classified a comparable metal (steel) pillbox as an article of base metal (K84936, in force), while pocket-carried pill cases have gone to 4202.39 (N335841).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "4202.39.90.00", "reason": "CBP has placed pocket-type pill cases here (N335841), but these are stated to be intended primarily for home use rather than carried in pocket or handbag; arguable"}, {"code": "4202.99.90.00", "reason": "alternative if treated as a 4202 'similar container' not carried on the person; ruling facts stress aluminum essential character and household use, pointing to chapter 76"}, {"code": "7616.99.51", "reason": "other articles of aluminum; heading 7615 more specifically covers household articles"}, {"code": "7615.10.71", "reason": "cooking and kitchen ware; a pill case is not kitchen ware"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363777", "reference_source": "ruling:N363777", "product": "The item under consideration is described as the Gabi System, which is a non-invasive, child-friendly, wearable device designed for continuous monitoring of pulse rate and blood volume variations. Utilizing advanced photoplethysmography (PPG) technology, the system employs red, infrared, and green LEDs to capture micro-fluctuations in blood circulation, translating optical absorption data into precise Pulse Rate (PR) and Oxygen Saturation (SpO2) metrics. This physiological data is securely transmitted in real-time via Bluetooth to mobile and cloud-based platforms, allowing parents and clinicians to monitor cardiovascular trends and review historical waveforms without disrupting the child’s daily routine or rest. The system is imported with the Gabi sensor, four various sized textile Gabi bands, a tablet with Gabi monitor app (to start and stop a recording of physiological parameters measured by the Gabi Sensor, displays measuring information, and transfers collected data to the Gabi Cloud via Wi-Fi), two charging cables, two power adapters, a quick setup guide and a set of instructions.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363777, gold code 9029.20.4080:\ned in real-time via Bluetooth to mobile and cloud-based platforms, allowing parents and clinicians to monitor cardiovascular trends and review historical waveforms without disrupting the child’s daily routine or rest. The system is imported with the Gabi sensor, four various sized textile Gabi bands, a tablet with Gabi monitor app (to start and stop a recording of physiological parameters measured by the Gabi Sensor, displays measuring information, and transfers collected data to the Gabi Cloud via Wi-Fi), two charging cables, two power adapters, a quick setup guide and a set of instructions. The applicable subheading for the Gabi System will be 9029.20.4080, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Revolution counters, production counters, taximeters, odometers, pedometers and the like; speedometers and tachometers, other than those of heading 9014 or 9015; stroboscopes; parts and accessories thereof: Speedometers and tachometers; stroboscopes: Other speedometers and tachometers: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Jason Christie at jason.m.christie@cbp.d", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9018.19.95.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9018 covers electro-diagnostic instruments and apparatus used in medical sciences, including apparatus for functional exploratory examination such as pulse oximeters", "GRI 3(b): the Gabi sensor, which performs the measurement, gives the system its essential character; bands, tablet, cables and adapters are ancillary", "GRI 6: 9018.19 other electro-diagnostic apparatus; 9018.19.95 other; 9018.19.95.50 other apparatus"], "rationale": "The Gabi sensor is a wearable pulse oximeter that measures pulse rate and oxygen saturation by photoplethysmography for monitoring by parents and clinicians. CBP classifies pulse oximeters in 9018.19.95 as electro-diagnostic apparatus (H338599, in force). The sensor performs the core function and gives the system its essential character, so the system falls in 9018.19.95.50 (other apparatus).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9018.19.95.60", "reason": "the sensor is a complete apparatus, not a part or accessory"}, {"code": "9031.80.80", "reason": "consumer wellness wearables go there; this device measures SpO2/pulse for medical monitoring and is covered by heading 9018"}, {"code": "8517.62.00", "reason": "Bluetooth transmission is ancillary to the medical measuring function"}, {"code": "8471.30.01", "reason": "the tablet is an accessory to the system; if CBP treats the items as not a set, the tablet would be classified separately"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363849", "reference_source": "ruling:N363849", "product": "The items under consideration are described as LPD strainers (models LPD0300, LPD1000, and LPD2400), which are engineered to protect downstream fluid system components, such as pumps, valves, and heat exchangers, by removing particulate matter and debris from process media. These devices utilize an internal stainless-steel perforated screen, housed within a robust body constructed of cast iron or ductile iron depending on the specific model size. The strainers feature a specialized internal geometry and an oversized screen that significantly reduces pressure drop compared to conventional models. The units, which contain no moving parts, are suitable for horizontal or vertical installation in water, oil, gas, and other compatible fluid-service applications, and are equipped with integral flanges, a hinged bolted cover plate, and blowoff plugs to facilitate maintenance and operation. Each of the models perform the same function but are differentiated by their size, shape and composition.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363849, gold code 8421.29.0065:\nuctile iron depending on the specific model size. The strainers feature a specialized internal geometry and an oversized screen that significantly reduces pressure drop compared to conventional models. The units, which contain no moving parts, are suitable for horizontal or vertical installation in water, oil, gas, and other compatible fluid-service applications, and are equipped with integral flanges, a hinged bolted cover plate, and blowoff plugs to facilitate maintenance and operation. Each of the models perform the same function but are differentiated by their size, shape and composition. The applicable subheading for the LPD strainers (models LPD0300, LPD1000, and LPD2400) will be 8421.29.0065, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Centrifuges, including centrifugal dryers; filtering or purifying machinery and apparatus, for liquids or gases; parts thereof: Filtering or purifying machinery and apparatus for liquids: Other: Other: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Jason Christie at jason.m.christie@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. For", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8421.29.00.65", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8421 covers filtering or purifying machinery and apparatus for liquids or gases; Section XVI covers apparatus without moving parts", "GRI 6: 8421.2 filtering apparatus for liquids; not solely for water (8421.21) as used with water, oil and other fluids; 8421.29.00 other", "GRI 6 statistical: not refrigerant recovery, oil-separation, or hydraulic fluid power filter; 8421.29.00.65 other"], "rationale": "The LPD strainers remove particulate matter from process fluids by passing them through a perforated stainless-steel screen, which is filtering apparatus of heading 8421. Because they are used with water, oil and other fluids rather than water alone, they fall in 8421.29.00 (other filtering apparatus for liquids). CBP classified Y-strainers in 8421.29.00 in HQ 964174 (in force), and the residual statistical line 8421.29.00.65 applies.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8421.21.00", "reason": "not designed solely for filtering or purifying water; used for oil, gas and other fluids"}, {"code": "8421.39", "reason": "principally used with liquid process media; gas service is secondary"}, {"code": "7325.10 / 7326.90", "reason": "cast iron article; heading 8421 is a more specific provision for filtering apparatus"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363928", "reference_source": "ruling:N363928", "product": "The product under consideration is a manually operated folding utility knife, item number D280180. It is designed for general cutting applications, such as cutting cardboard, plastic, and other common materials. This knife features a locking mechanism that secures a replaceable standard utility blade in position, as well as a quick-change system that allows the user to easily replace blades. The knife is made primarily of zinc alloy and stainless steel. The knife will come with a replacement blade and will include an attached screwdriver.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363928, gold code 8211.93.0035:\ntection BV. An image was provided in lieu of a sample. The product under consideration is a manually operated folding utility knife, item number D280180. It is designed for general cutting applications, such as cutting cardboard, plastic, and other common materials. This knife features a locking mechanism that secures a replaceable standard utility blade in position, as well as a quick-change system that allows the user to easily replace blades. The knife is made primarily of zinc alloy and stainless steel. The knife will come with a replacement blade and will include an attached screwdriver. The applicable subheading for this folding utility knife, item number D280180, will be 8211.93.0035, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “[k]nives with cutting blades, serrated or not (including pruning knives), other than knives of heading 8208, and blades and other base metal parts thereof: [o]ther: [k]nives having other than fixed blades: [p]en knives, pocket knives and other knives which have folding blades.” The column one, general rate of duty is 3 cents each plus 5.4 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the CBP Regulations (19 CFR 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Kristophe", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8211.93.00.35", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8211 covers knives with cutting blades, serrated or not; chapter 82 note excludes nothing relevant; replaceable-blade knife is not a tool of 8205", "GRI 3(b): the attached screwdriver is subsidiary; the folding knife gives essential character; the spare blade is classified with the knife", "GRI 6: 8211.93 knives having other than fixed blades; 8211.93.00.35 pen knives, pocket knives and other knives which have folding blades"], "rationale": "The item is a folding utility knife with a locking, replaceable blade, which is a knife having other than a fixed blade in heading 8211. The attached screwdriver and spare blade do not change its essential character as a knife. CBP classified a materially identical manually operated folding utility knife in 8211.93.0035 (N361367, in force).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8211.93.00.60", "reason": "the blade folds into the handle, so the folding-blade line is more specific"}, {"code": "8211.93.00.31", "reason": "not a two-handled multi-use tool"}, {"code": "8205.40", "reason": "screwdriver is a minor attached feature; knife function predominates"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363949", "reference_source": "ruling:N363949", "product": "The submitted article, identified as item number D280065, is a unisex baseball-style cap constructed of 100 percent cotton woven fabric. The cap features a multi-panel crown, a curbed peak, and an embroidered “FENTO” logo on the front of the crown.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363949, gold code 6505.00.2060:\n 1, 2026 CLA-2-65:OT:RR:NC:N1:358 CATEGORY: Classification TARIFF NO.: 6505.00.2060 Sean Scarbrough Taggart International LTD 2825 Lewis Speedway, Unit 108 Saint Augustine, FL 32084 RE: The tariff classification of a baseball-style cap from China Dear Mr. Scarbrough: In your letter dated August 2, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. The submitted article, identified as item number D280065, is a unisex baseball-style cap constructed of 100 percent cotton woven fabric. The cap features a multi-panel crown, a curbed peak, and an embroidered “FENTO” logo on the front of the crown. The applicable subheading for item D280065 will be 6505.00.2060, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for hats and other headgear, knitted or crocheted, or made up from lace, felt or other textile fabric, in the piece (but not in strips), whether or not lined or trimmed: other: other: of cotton, flax or both: not knitted: certified hand-loomed and folklore products; and headwear of cotton: other. The general rate of duty will be 7.5 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Katherine Souffront at kath", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6505.00.20.60", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 6505 covers hats and other headgear made up from textile fabric in the piece", "GRI 6: 6505.00 other; of cotton, flax or both; not knitted; 6505.00.20 headwear of cotton; 6505.00.20.60 other (not for babies)"], "rationale": "The cap is headgear made up from woven cotton fabric in the piece, which falls in heading 6505. Because it is of cotton and not knitted, and is not for babies, the line is 6505.00.20.60. The embroidered logo does not affect classification. CBP classified a cotton brushed twill six-panel cap in the equivalent cotton, not-knitted provision (L81714, in force; its old code 6505.90.2060 is the prior numbering of this line).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6505.00.15", "reason": "cap is of woven, not knitted, fabric"}, {"code": "6505.00.20.30", "reason": "not for babies"}, {"code": "6505.00.60", "reason": "that line is for man-made fiber headwear; this cap is cotton"}]}}
